A||N||7||DaveL||MAP Sensor Makes No Sense||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||02-15-2000||02:35 PM||||I've been having some idle problems on my 87 SE 2.5 DIS, so I thought I'd try your trick of undoing the electrical connector to the MAP sensor. But my car wouldn't start at first, then ran briefly before stalling. Could that indicate an ECM problem?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||02-15-2000||02:45 PM||||With the MAP sesor disconnected the engine will shut down after a few moments. If you are having Idle problems the first sensor to check is the IAC. If it is dirty it will cause your idle to be erratic, very low or "floating". As for IAC is concerned sometimes you can just remove it clean it and the hole it goes in with carb cleaner and this fixes it. If it's clean the IAC could be bad. Note if you replace IAC the car may idle high. You need to drive it over 30mph for the ECM to reset the sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||02-15-2000||06:36 PM||||tricks that work with distributor motors don't always work on DIS.<P>check the obvious like vacuum leaks and ignition. Never use Bosch type platinum plugs with the thin center electrode. DIS hates them. Pretty much everyone here that's put them in a DIS motor has fried the electrode out of the plug.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||02-15-2000||07:19 PM||||Wow. I just got of my email and sa all the posts on the FieroONE list saying that Accel wires and Bosch Platinums are a bad choice, now I see this. Is someone tryin to tell me somethin?  Is the Platinums a bad call on the V6 too, or is it just a 4 cyl thing? I am using the super stock spiral wound wires (8mm) and Bosch Platinums.  Has anyone had probs with these products on a V6? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-15-2000||07:26 PM||||Stock Distributor motors don't generaly seem to have problems with plugs. DIS coil packs produce more current than regular coils. Hotter after market coils could produce similar problems as DIS experiences with Bosch Platinum and Platinum +4 plugs.<P>Frankly I find most aftermarket "performance" wires don't provide any better results than the Borg Warner helical core wires you can get at any pep boys for a heck of allot less. The B/W's have lifetime replacement even. The set on my DIS motor is over 25Kmiles old and are just as solid as when new.<P>I did run my DIS motor once w/o the MAP. The wire was loose and fell off. It ran but Really Baddly.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||02-15-2000||09:45 PM||||Ogre, I am also using an Accel Super Coil. Have I just mixed myself up a recipe for disaster?  How will this likely manifest itself when it goes bad?<BR>I believe you about the BorgWarners being good wires.  To tell the truth, I bought the shiny red accel wires cuz they look so cool. What a fashion victim,huh?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gokart||02-16-2000||05:36 AM||||I've had Bosch platinum plugs in my GT for over 6 months now and have had no problems. They still feel as strong as the day I put them in. I don't know weather or not they are the ones with the thin electrodes though.||||reg||2
Z||000007||theogre||02-16-2000||08:55 AM||||If it's been running platinum plugs more than a week or 2 w/o problems then you don't have a problem. The bunt electrode problem seems to only be common on DIS motors.<P>The accel wires and coil should not be a problem. they just cost allot more for no real function other than red ones that match the V6 look nice. The B/W wires are gray, but on my black and bare aluminum DIS 4 that actually looks nice. The only time I've ever seen "performance" wires do any good is in race motors turning well over 6-7000RPM. Aftermarket Coils can help motors at lower RPM. The main reason is that stock coils often don't charge as fast or as deep as after market ones. This can make the stock system weak above 4-5000rpm. (I'm not even talking about motors under boost here.)<P>There are 3 types of "platinum" spark plug that I've seen. The original Bosch Paltinums use a thin center electrode, 22-24AWG, in a ceramic tower, but continue to use a traditional ground. The Bosch Platinum +4 is a "face fire" plug with the same electrode, but uses 4 gound terminals around the perimiter of the plug. The third type is a platinum tiped traditional plug, autolite as i recall, which is a traditional design with platinum only on the tip of the center electrode. I have not heard of problems with this third type of plug. Only with the Bosch plugs, and then only in GM DIS motors.||||reg||1
